{
  "name": "hold_choke",
  "note": "(anim-rework S6 keys, real-world standard, keyed by hand; never traced; M5) The knockout from the hold, a 5.5 s one-shot keyed at guard_choke_sag's own frames (0, 1, 5, 10, 14, 18, 22, 28, 33, 40, 47 of 48; play the phase min(t / 5.5, 1) x 47 / 48 like his): 0-0.6 s the right forearm tightens across his throat and the left hand comes up onto her right wrist, her chest drawing back; 0.6-1.2 s his knees give and she sinks with him; 1.2-1.6 s limp: both forearms slide under his arms onto his upper chest (a rescue hold), she bends her knees and steps back; 1.6-2.6 s she lowers him through a deep squat to sitting, staying behind him; 1.2-3.9 s she steps back with him (the pelvis dz from -16 to -92 cm, centred on him, knees spread either side of his head: his neck passes her start position by 1.6 s and his head lies 0.81 m behind it; the wiring should move her root instead) as she lays him back, bent over his shoulders; 3.9-5.5 s she lets go and settles in a low crouch at his head, hands by her knees. Controlled throughout, no slam. Not mirrored. Angles in degrees in the body frame (x right, y up, z forward): pitch about x (+ the top forward), yaw about y (+ turns right), roll about z (+ the right side up). Pelvis offsets in cm from the standing rest. Arms: flex (+ forward, chest frame), abd (+ out), elbow (+ bent), inward (the forearm turned in); wrist (anim-rig, RIG.md) yaw / pitch / roll in the forearm frame, right hand as written, the left mirrored. The arms were solved onto the guard's contacts (his clips posed at the game's hold placement: 0.32 m ahead, 0.1 m to her left, his 1.78 m against her 1.68 m) and written as plain angles: edit these numbers directly. Legs (the Blender preview and the track only: the game's feet come from the foot planner): flex (+ the knee forward), add (+ the foot in), knee (+ bent), ankle (+ the toes up).",
